                <pb facs="0599" n="514" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg514"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.73/11: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-01-11T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 11,
                                1919, 4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>4017. Department has today sent the following instruction to the American Chargé
                    d’Affaires in Paris for communication to the French Government:</p>
                <p>[Here follows substance of telegram printed <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >supra.</hi>]</p>
                <p>Please advise the British Foreign Office of the position of this Department and
                    express to the Minister of Foreign Affairs this Government’s abiding interest in
                    the welfare of Liberia. You will say that the Department cannot believe that the
                    British Government would regard with sympathy the grave step proposed by the
                    French Government which seems so wholly inconsistent with the principles for
                    which the associated governments have been contending.</p>
                <p>Cable the views of the British Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
